<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>12.0</LangVersion>
    <AssemblyName>VL.Mapsui</AssemblyName>
    <!--
      The VL node category comes from the namespace, not the assembly name. Keeping the two
      decoupled is deliberate, and is the same arrangement VL.GIS uses.
    -->
    <RootNamespace>VL.Mapsui</RootNamespace>
    <Version>0.0.1</Version>
    <Authors>VL.Mapsui Contributors</Authors>
    <Description>Mapsui as vvvv gamma nodes</Description>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

    <!--
      Put every NuGet dependency next to the built assembly. A class library does not do this
      by default: it emits VL.Mapsui.dll alone and leaves the host to resolve Mapsui, BruTile
      and Svg.Skia from deps.json plus the NuGet cache.

      vvvv does not resolve them that way when the project is pulled in through a
      ProjectDependency, and the failure is quiet and easy to misread: nodes whose signatures
      mention no Mapsui type work perfectly, while the ones returning or taking Mapsui.Map are
      simply greyed out - not red, not logged, no exception anywhere. The dividing line is the
      signature, which is what gives the cause away.
    -->
    <CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies>true</CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Both ship inside vvvv. ExcludeAssets="runtime" is what keeps our copies out of the output
      folder while still compiling against them; without it CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies below
      drops VL.Core.dll and VL.Core.Skia.dll next to our assembly, which is exactly the thing
      every note in VL.GIS warns against.

      Pinned to 2025.7.4, which is what is installed here, so the minimum vvvv is 7.4. The
      attributes this package needs ([Name], [SkipCategory]) arrived in 7.2, so 2025.7.2 would
      also do and would widen the audience; revisit when there is a reason to care.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="VL.Core" Version="2025.7.4" ExcludeAssets="runtime" />
    <PackageReference Include="VL.Core.Skia" Version="2025.7.4" ExcludeAssets="runtime" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Mapsui 4.1.9, not 5.x. 5.x needs SkiaSharp >= 3.119.2 and vvvv ships 2.88.8, which is an
      assembly-identity break nothing can paper over.

      SkiaSharp is deliberately NOT pinned here. Mapsui.Rendering.Skia requires
      >= 2.88.9 while vvvv ships 2.88.8, so pinning vvvv's version would fail restore with
      NU1605. Letting it resolve to 2.88.9 is correct and harmless: the whole 2.88.x line
      carries assembly version 2.88.0.0, so vvvv's copy satisfies the reference at runtime.
      Measured, not assumed - the same holds for HarfBuzzSharp (1.0.0.0 on both sides despite
      file versions 7.3.0.1 vs 7.3.0.3) and SkiaSharp.HarfBuzz (2.88.0.0 on both).

      Mapsui.Tiling is what pins BruTile to [5.0.6, 6.0.0), and it is the sole reason this
      package cannot be loaded alongside VL.GIS, which uses BruTile 6. That is an accepted
      trade for now; it dissolves when vvvv moves to SkiaSharp 3 and we can use Mapsui 5.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Mapsui" Version="4.1.9" />
    <PackageReference Include="Mapsui.Tiling" Version="4.1.9" />
    <!--
      Declared explicitly although Mapsui.Nts already drags it in. VL needs an upstream library to
      be a package in a package repository, not merely a restorable assembly: without that it
      cannot resolve the type, and a node whose signature mentions NetTopologySuite.Geometry is
      built with no working pins while every link to it is dropped - vvvvc exits 0 and nothing is
      red. build.ps1 installs what this file declares into deps\.

      2.6.0 rather than the 2.5.0 Mapsui.Nts asks for, so both repositories name the same version.
      The whole 2.x line carries assembly version 2.0.0.0, so the loader sees one identity either
      way; naming the same number removes the question of which folder wins.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="NetTopologySuite" Version="2.6.0" />
    <!--
      The neutral feature model - NetTopologySuite.Features.Feature plus AttributesTable - rather
      than a VLFeature of our own. Declared for the same reason NetTopologySuite above is: it
      already arrives transitively through Mapsui.Nts and compiles fine either way, but VL cannot
      resolve a type whose package is not present as a package, and that failure is silent. A node
      whose signature names Feature would be built with no working pins and every link to it
      dropped.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="NetTopologySuite.Features" Version="2.1.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Mapsui.Rendering.Skia" Version="4.1.9" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>